Shipping it: an installer and a launcher
========================================

The deliverable this project has always described -- "somebody on XP x64
downloads a release and runs one executable" -- against what is actually in the
tree today. Two programs are missing and one assumption turned out to be false.

The two programs are an installer, which lays down the files and builds the
Linux system on the machine it is installed on, and a launcher, which is a
window with options and a Start button in place of the six batch files
doc/runbook currently walks people through.

The false assumption is the subject of section 1, and it comes first because
nothing else matters if the binaries do not load.


1. The release does not run on Windows XP x64
----------------------------------------------

    colinux-daemon.exe      imports  api-ms-win-crt-{convert,environment,
                                     filesystem,heap,locale,math,private,
                                     runtime,stdio,string,utility}-l1-1-0.dll
    colinux-slirp-net-daemon.exe     the same, plus -time
    linux.sys                        ntoskrnl.exe, HAL.dll, NDIS.SYS

Those api-ms-win-crt-* names are the Universal CRT's API sets. The UCRT is a
Windows 10 component; Microsoft's redistributable of it supports Vista SP2
through 8.1 and has never supported XP. mingw-w64 16.1 links it by default, so
every userspace binary this port has produced requires a runtime that does not
exist on the operating system it targets. The driver is clean -- it imports
only kernel exports -- which is why the failure is invisible from the driver
side.

It is invisible from the test box too, and that is the part worth recording.
The M92p has one-core-api installed: E:\WINDOWS\system32 contains
kernelbase.dll, ucrtbase.dll and api-ms-win-core-processthreads-l1-1-0.dll,
none of which ship with a 5.2 kernel. That backport is also why Steam, six
CEF-based steamwebhelper processes, Supermium and the Rockstar launcher run on
a 2005 operating system. So the one machine that could have caught this was
the one machine constitutionally unable to.

The fix is a link flag, and the toolchain already has both halves:

    x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc h.c -o h.exe -mcrtdll=msvcrt-os
      -> DLL Name: KERNEL32.dll
         DLL Name: msvcrt.dll

gcc's own spec carries the selector -- `%{!mcrtdll=*:-lmsvcrt} %{mcrtdll=*:-l%*}`
-- and libmsvcrt-os.a is the genuine msvcrt.dll import library sitting beside
the UCRT-forwarding default. libmingwex.a has no references to
__stdio_common_* at all, so it fills the gaps rather than pulling the UCRT back
in by another route. Subsystem version is already 5.2 on all three binaries and
stays there; _WIN32_WINNT goes to 0x0502 so the headers stop declaring things
the target does not have.

What this needs alongside it is a gate, because a silent dependency is exactly
what it was. `make dist` refuses to package a binary that imports any
api-ms-win-* or kernelbase.dll, or that declares a subsystem above 5.2. It is
an objdump parse and a comparison, and it is the only mechanical check between
"it builds" and "it loads on the target".

And the consequence for testing: the M92p can no longer tell anyone whether a
release works. Anything that would fail on a clean machine still runs there. A
stock XP x64 VM on the development host -- no coLinux in it, only "does the
payload load" -- is the honest reference, and it costs one qemu install.


2. What ships
--------------

    colinux-daemon.exe    every daemon mode, including the slirp bridge
    mocolinux.exe         the launcher and the first-run wizard
    linux.sys             the driver
    vmlinux               the guest kernel
    root-seed.img         a minimal Manjaro that can build the real one
    an X server           see section 5

Seven executables become one. Six of the current seven are either dead on this
port (colinux-console-nt cannot attach to an x86-64 guest at all), unused
(ndis, serial, net), or diagnostic (debug-daemon, which additionally has a
use-after-free in its drain path -- see TODO).

The one that cannot simply be deleted is colinux-slirp-net-daemon.exe.  It is
linked at 0x400000 with IMAGE_FILE_LARGE_ADDRESS_AWARE cleared, deliberately,
because slirp stores pointers in 32-bit queue-link fields and its structure
overlays only land on wire format when SIZEOF_CHAR_P is 8 -- the reasoning is
in the R7 commit and in slirp_config.h. Folding the bridge into
colinux-daemon.exe therefore means confining that binary to 2 GB as well.
Nothing in the daemon wants a high address -- it streams vmlinux in 32 KB
chunks and never maps guest RAM into user space, which lives in the driver's
contiguous allocations -- so this should cost nothing, and tools/pe-clear-laa.py
already exists to do it. It is a claim to verify, not a fact.

The launcher stays a second executable rather than a mode of the first. A PE
has one subsystem: a single binary either flashes a console when it opens the
GUI, or needs AttachConsole for its daemon modes and hands the prompt back
while still printing, which is precisely the behaviour doc/runbook's foreground
invocations depend on not happening.


3. The installer
-----------------

NSIS, and it does not drive the guest.

    1. Preflight       x64, 5.2 or later, Administrators, NTFS, free space.
    2. Extract         the payload above, uncompressed in the tree -- NSIS's
                       LZMA takes a mostly-empty ext4 down hard, so there is no
                       zstd step and nothing to expand at runtime.
    3. Driver          create the service and start it. --install-driver
                       currently reports success without starting anything,
                       which is its state after every reboot; everything
                       downstream then fails with "manager open: cannot find
                       the file". That is a TODO item and a small fix.
    4. Hand off        mocolinux.exe --first-run, and wait for it.
    5. Finish          shortcuts, uninstaller, and a reboot prompt (section 6).

Steps 2 through 5 of the request -- stand the guest up, run the build, verify
-- are deliberately not NSIS's. That work takes twenty to forty-five minutes
over the network, needs progress, cancel, retry and resume, and speaks to the
guest over a TCP console. All of that already has to exist in the launcher.


4. The first-run wizard
------------------------

This is doc/runbook section B, unattended.

  * Create root-manjaro.img sparse and in place. Never transferred: the agent
    buffers a pushed image and the deploy reads it back to verify, which is
    tens of gigabytes of churn and has taken the box down.

  * Spawn --boot with the seed on cobd0 and the target on cobd1, plus --slirp
    and --console, as children with our own pipes and no inherited console
    handles. That last part is not fastidiousness: `start` hands its standard
    handles to the child whatever you do with them, the caller then waits for
    an end-of-file that cannot come while the child lives, and three separate
    launchers in this tree have wedged that way. A Job Object with
    KILL_ON_JOB_CLOSE is the backstop, not the mechanism -- every daemon now
    exits on its own when the guest goes.

  * Wait for the guest. Half a minute is normal. "Connection refused" means
    the console has not bound yet and no prompt means the guest has not reached
    one; neither is a failure and the wizard must not report them as one.

  * Let the guest drive the build. The seed carries the script and a systemd
    unit that runs it against cobd1 and prints machine-readable markers --
    MOCO:STAGE 4/9, MOCO:PCT 41, MOCO:OK, MOCO:FAIL <reason> -- and the wizard
    only reads. The alternative is pushing shell through a serial console, and
    mkmanjarorootfs.sh already carries a comment about layered quoting eating a
    dollar sign, which is the failure that argument loses on.

  * Resume rather than restart. The script mke2fs -F's unconditionally today,
    so a run that dies at package 600 throws away three gigabytes of cache. A
    mirror dropping mid-install is the likeliest thing a user will hit, and
    keeping the filesystem lets pacman skip what it already has.

  * Verify before believing it, because a block-layer bug once produced an
    image whose own package database was binary noise: pacman -Qk against the
    mounted target, the script's own clean-unmount line, e2fsck -n, and the
    presence of systemd, fstab and the hvc0 getty override.

  * Finish with poweroff and never with --stop. Nothing else unmounts, and
    ext4 in data=ordered journals metadata and not contents, so a file written
    seconds earlier comes back as a correctly-named inode with no data. The
    boot child exiting is the flush.


5. The X server
----------------

The screenshot in the README -- KDE applications in XP windows -- is what most
people will come for, so the aim is that it works after one installer and no
further reading.

The X server on the test box cannot deliver that. It is VcXsrv 1.20.14, built
January 2022 with linker 14.29, and its PE declares MajorSubsystemVersion 6.0;
a 5.2 loader refuses an image that asks for Vista. It also wants vcruntime140
and the UCRT. It runs on the M92p for the same reason our own daemons do, and
for no other reason.

Downloading one-core-api during installation does not rescue it, and the reason
is circular: stock XP x64's schannel tops out at TLS 1.0, GitHub has required
1.2 since 2018, and NSIS's download plugins go through WinINet. The machine
that needs the backport is the machine that cannot fetch it. Bundling a
self-contained TLS downloader to solve that is more work than bundling the
thing being downloaded. And installing it unasked is the highest-liability act
available here -- it replaces core system DLLs and requires a reboot, so a
machine it breaks was broken by our installer.

So the X server has to be one that XP x64 can load unaided, and there is one:
VcXsrv 1.14.2.1, the last release before 1.14.3.0 dropped XP support.
Measured, by extracting the installer and reading the headers rather than by
trying it:

    every PE in the installer     subsystem 5.2
    vcxsrv.exe imports            ADVAPI32 GDI32 KERNEL32 ole32 OPENGL32
                                  SHELL32 SHLWAPI USER32 WS2_32, plus
                                  MSVCR100 and its own bundled X libraries
    MSVCR100.dll, MSVCP100.dll    shipped inside the installer directory

MSVCR100 is the Visual C++ 2010 runtime, which supports XP, and it does not
have to be installed separately because the installer lays it down beside the
binaries. Nothing in the set needs a backported API.

One flag has to go, and it is not a loss. xstart.bat passes +iglx, which
exists because xorg-server 1.17 disabled indirect GLX by default. This server
is xorg-server 1.14 and predates that: the string "iglx" does not appear in
the binary at all, indirect GLX is simply on, and passing the option would be
a fatal-error dialog on a headless machine. -wgl, -multiwindow, -clipboard,
-ac and -notrayicon are all present. tools/xstart1142.bat is xstart.bat
without it.

Verified end to end on the M92p with the guest of the commit that made the
release loadable: installed to its own directory with /S /D so the 1.20 build
already there was left alone, launched through WScript.Shell, and then from
the guest --

    xdpyinfo    vendor string HC-Consult, vendor release 11402001
    xrandr      Screen 0: current 1600 x 900
    xterm       a rootless window with an XP title bar, showing
                Linux mocolinux 7.1.5 #62 PREEMPT_DYNAMIC x86_64

-- which is the README's screenshot reproduced on a stack with no one-core-api
in it anywhere.

The one piece of friction it surfaced: Windows Firewall prompts about vcxsrv
the first time, because the server listens on 6000 on every interface. The
guest is unaffected either way -- slirp rewrites 10.0.2.2:0 into a connection
to the host's own loopback, which the firewall does not police, and the xterm
above appeared with the dialog still on screen unanswered. Keeping it blocked
is the right answer, so the installer should make it rather than ask: XP's
`netsh firewall add allowedprogram ... mode=DISABLE` puts the program on the
exception list in the disabled state, which suppresses the prompt without
opening anything.

The installer still looks for an X server already on the machine and prefers
it, and records in its own registry key whether the bundled one was installed,
so the uninstaller removes only what it put there.


6. The reboot at the end, and why it is there for now
------------------------------------------------------

The installer boots the guest once to build the image, which makes the user's
first real launch the second boot on a loaded driver -- the case doc/runbook
calls "the one that governs the whole day". Guest RAM is freed and re-hunted in
unbroken 32 MB runs every run, so the second boot grinds the host with memory
showing as available throughout.

An installer that ends in a reboot is unremarkable, so that is the stopgap. The
fix is in TODO and is worth doing afterwards rather than first: allocate the
guest's RAM once per driver load and reuse it, with co_kload_free keeping the
blocks and a new co_kload_release_memory dropping them from the unload path.
It is the teardown path, which produced a bugcheck 0xC2 and a host freeze in a
single evening, so it deserves its own change and its own audit. When it lands,
the reboot prompt and the launcher's warning both come out.


7. The launcher
----------------

Native Win32 through the existing mingw-w64 target: no .NET, no Python, one
file, and a build.comake.py entry like everything else.

Main: driver and guest state, uptime, memory; Start, Stop, Force stop; Open
terminal; Launch application; a log tail.

Options, persisted beside the executable: memory in MB, cobd0 through cobd3,
init, networking, X server and its flags, console port, diagnostics, and the
advanced pair --no-copic and --max-switches. Plus "Rebuild the Linux system",
which is the wizard again.

The rules it has to encode are the ones doc/runbook spends its length on. The
X server must exist before any X client, because an X client with no server
produces no error at all and simply never appears -- that cost an hour once.
Stop means poweroff and then wait; Force stop is --stop; nothing is ever
killed, because taskkill on a thread inside the driver closes its handles
underneath kernel-mode code and that is the recurring 0x50. One instance, and
one of each child. And, until section 6 is fixed, a warning on the second start
since Windows last booted.


8. Order
---------

  0. -mcrtdll=msvcrt-os, _WIN32_WINNT=0x0502, the import gate, a reference VM.
  1. Fold slirp into colinux-daemon.exe and clear LAA; --install-driver starts
     the service; the shared modules both programs need -- console client,
     child supervision, service control, configuration.
  2. tools/mkseedrootfs.sh, the in-guest setup unit and its progress markers,
     and the seed built here rather than inside a guest -- which also settles
     the TODO entry saying the script has never run on a build host, the one
     case that matters for shipping. A swapfile goes into mkmanjarorootfs.sh
     while it is open: the guest measures 97% system time in permanent reclaim
     without one, and that is most of what people will call slowness.
  3. mocolinux.exe -- the wizard first, since the launcher reuses all of it.
  4. NSIS and a `make dist` that assembles and checks the payload, which is
     also the answer to TODO's "the release artifacts are not built by
     anything".
